\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Counting coordinates for a selected law}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{Let #{B:\mathbb R^r\to H} be an isometric inclusion onto the
selected space of the [selected subspace law](fdip-005B), so
#{BB^{\dagger}=P}. In this basis
##{B^{\dagger}C_PB=B^{\dagger}GB,\qquad [S_P]_B=(B^{\dagger}GB)^{-1}.}
For another such basis #{B'=BU} with #{U\in O(r)}, the precision
matrix becomes #{U^{\dagger}[S_P]_BU}. The operator #{S_P} is unchanged.}

\p{Take orthonormal bases of the retained and eliminated summands.
In those bases the blocks defined by the inclusions have matrix
##{Q=\begin{pmatrix}A&K\\K^{\dagger}&D\end{pmatrix},}
and the retained precision is the Schur complement
#{A-KD^{-1}K^{\dagger}}.}

\p{A partial formal correspondence is supplied by TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.Probability.map_affine_multivariateGaussian}.
It allows finite real coordinate spaces, a positive semidefinite
covariance and an arbitrary rectangular linear map. Set its mean
and shift to zero, its covariance to #{G=Q^{-1}>0}, and its matrix
to #{B^{\dagger}}. After the [precision-density identification](fdip-000B),
the result gives the coordinate image law with covariance #{B^{\dagger}GB}.
It does not require full row rank. Positivity of this compressed
covariance, its inverse precision, the isometric identification
with the selected space, and the graph data are the additional
arguments of the theorem.}
